megolm — provenance note
=========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `signal`/
`mls`/`bip340`/`adaptor`/`bolt8` modules — self-contained protocol/crypto
modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

megolm — clean-room implementation of Matrix's Megolm group ratchet,
specified in a single public document published by the Matrix.org
Foundation:

  - Megolm: "Megolm group ratchet", https://gitlab.matrix.org/matrix-org/olm/-/blob/master/docs/megolm.md
    (Apache License 2.0, per the spec document's own "License" section)

A public specification's text is not copyrightable expression (merger
doctrine, CONVENTIONS.md §5) — implementing it, however closely, is not
"derived from" any third party's code.

**Design references (not ported, but read and relied on)**: the Megolm
spec's own "advance the ratchet" description is a piecewise per-part
recurrence, not directly an algorithm for fast-forwarding to an arbitrary
index without visiting every intermediate one — settling the exact
cascade mechanics (which HMAC argument is the key vs. the message; how
"advance to an arbitrary target" decomposes into per-byte-position
rehash counts; the 32-bit-counter wraparound edge case) required reading
two reference implementations as design references, per CONVENTIONS.md
§5's "design reference" category:

  - **libolm** (C, Apache License 2.0) — `src/megolm.c`/`include/olm/
    megolm.h` (the `megolm_advance`/`megolm_advance_to`/`rehash_part`
    functions) and `tests/test_megolm.cpp`/`tests/test_message.cpp`/
    `tests/test_group_session.cpp` (numeric test vectors — see "Test
    vectors" below). Fetched from
    https://gitlab.matrix.org/matrix-org/olm (the canonical upstream).
  - **vodozemac** (Rust, Apache License 2.0) — `src/megolm/ratchet.rs`,
    `src/megolm/message.rs`, `src/megolm/session_keys.rs`,
    `src/cipher/mod.rs` (confirms the same cascade algorithm, the
    HKDF/AES-CBC/HMAC cipher instantiation, and the wire-format byte
    layouts independently of libolm's C). Fetched from
    https://github.com/matrix-org/vodozemac (the current Matrix.org
    reference implementation, successor to libolm).

This module's `ratchet.zig`/`cipher.zig`/`message.zig`/`session_key.zig`/
`session.zig` are a from-scratch Zig implementation of the algorithm and
formats those two implementations were read to confirm — no libolm C or
vodozemac Rust source was copied, ported, or transliterated line-by-line
into this module. `std.crypto.kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256`/`std.crypto.auth.
hmac.sha2.HmacSha256`/`std.crypto.sign.Ed25519`/`std.base64` (all MIT,
part of Zig itself) and the sibling `aescbc` module (this repository's
own, MIT) supply every cryptographic primitive; no new hash, HMAC, HKDF,
signature, or AES-CBC implementation is written here.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Test vectors
------------

`src/kat_test.zig` embeds NUMERIC FACTS (ratchet byte states at specific
indices, and a real session-key + message byte pair) from libolm's own
test suite — not copyrightable expression, per the same merger-doctrine
reasoning as the spec text itself, and consistent with how `signal`'s
NOTICE treats libsignal's published test vector. Specifically:

  - `tests/test_megolm.cpp`'s `Megolm::advance`/`Megolm::advance
    wraparound`/`Megolm::advance overflow by one`/`Megolm::advance
    overflow` test cases — the `random_bytes` seed and `expected1`/
    `expected2`/`expected3` byte arrays, extracted programmatically (see
    `kat_test.zig`'s module doc comment for the exact reproduction
    command) rather than hand-transcribed.
  - `tests/test_message.cpp`'s "Group message encode test" — the
    `_olm_encode_group_message` header bytes for `(version=3,
    counter=200, ciphertext_len=10)`.
  - `tests/test_group_session.cpp`'s "Inbound group session export/
    import" and "Invalid signature group message" test cases — two real,
    complete `session_key`/`message` base64 byte pairs, plus the exact
    single-byte tamper the latter test applies (the message's final byte,
    part of its Ed25519 signature, set to ASCII `'E'`).

All of the above were fetched with `curl` from
`gitlab.matrix.org/matrix-org/olm` (see `kat_test.zig` for the exact
URLs) and extracted with small Python regex scripts over the raw `.cpp`
source text, not read and retyped by hand — a first by-eye transcription
of the 128-byte ASCII ratchet seed (`"0123456789ABCDEF"` repeated 8
times) misread it as containing `"...ABDEF..."` (a missing `C`); the
programmatic extraction caught this before it reached a test, and is the
version actually embedded in `kat_test.zig`.

**Independent re-derivation**: the two full session-key + message vectors
above were additionally decoded, signature-verified, key-derived, and
decrypted with a THIRD, unrelated toolchain — Python's `cryptography`
library (AES-256-CBC, HKDF-SHA256), the Python standard library's
`hmac`/`hashlib` (HMAC-SHA256), and PyNaCl 1.5 (Ed25519) — sharing no
code with libolm, vodozemac, or this module's Zig implementation, before
being trusted and embedded as test vectors.

Status of this pass
--------------------

**Complete** for the scope stated in README.md: `ratchet.zig`'s hash
ratchet (`advanceStep`/`advanceToUnchecked`/guarded `advanceTo`),
`cipher.zig`'s HKDF+AES-256-CBC+HMAC-SHA256 layer, `message.zig`'s wire
message codec, `session_key.zig`'s session-sharing/session-export
formats, and `session.zig`'s `OutboundSession`/`InboundGroupSession` are
implemented and tested — green in Debug, byte-exact against libolm's own
test vectors where they exist (see SPEC.md's anchoring-grade table for
exactly which areas that covers and which are self-consistency only).
Olm (the pairwise partner protocol), the Matrix event-JSON layer, and key
backup are out of scope — see README.md.
